  "notes": [
    {
      "title": "Director's statement",
      "pinned": true,
      "body": "Low Tide Radio is about listening. The village has told one story about Mariamma for twenty years because nobody heard the other one. Every department serves that idea: sound leads, picture follows. We shoot the 1999 scenes on the same lenses as the present, graded warmer and grainier, never as a separate film."
    },
    {
      "title": "Sound philosophy",
      "pinned": true,
      "body": "The reel is a character. Farhan records every playback live on set from the real Nagra with the restored tape transfer. No score under any scene where the reel plays. Static is designed per scene from real VHF recordings made at Munambam in December 2026."
    },
    {
      "title": "Safety plan: water and night work",
      "pinned": false,
      "body": "Breakwater (scene 24): harness on all crew past the yellow marker, two safety divers, rescue boat on channel 16, ambulance at the harbour road. Sea unit (scenes 1, 34): life jackets for everyone, stop if swell is over 1.5 m. Rain towers use fresh water from a tanker, never seawater, near electrics."
    },
    {
      "title": "Child performers",
      "pinned": false,
      "body": "Ameya (YOUNG ANJALI) and Gauri (MEENU): permits from the district labour office received 2026-12-20. No work after 20:00 without a written exemption. Sister Lissy tutors and chaperones. Night scenes 2 and 32 are shot first on day 2 with a 19:30 wrap."
    },
    {
      "title": "Malayalam dialogue and subtitles",
      "pinned": false,
      "body": "Scene 10 is in Malayalam with English subtitles. Subtitle text lives as notes in the screenplay. Anitha (script supervisor) checks every take against the Malayalam line; Divya Mathew coaches the pronunciation of the 1999 radio phrases."
    }
  ],
